          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-17563a9e79376c8c\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Mathilde’s written “Je vous aime” is primarily an explicit avowal of her loving state. Because it concludes assurances that Julien can rely on her devotion and steadfastness in adversity, it also carries moderate force as a confirmation of relational commitment. Nothing indicates that the words escape as a reflex-like affective discharge; urgency and intensity alone do not establish E.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the degree of P: the commitment is stated most explicitly in the preceding sentence, while “Je vous aime” may either ratify that undertaking or function only as its emotional explanation.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Mathilde writes the utterance directly to Julien as the closing sentence of an urgent letter.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Mathilde writes amid her father’s attempt to prevent her union with Julien. Her call for him to sacrifice everything, coupled with her promise to remain devoted and steadfast in adversity, frames the love avowal as serious allegiance under pressure rather than casual affection. Julien’s immediate, anxious journey and Mathilde’s subsequent embrace reinforce the letter’s practical and emotional seriousness, but do not make the utterance expressive/reflexive in the specified sense.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Mathilde’s letter ends with the direct statement “Je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional force: the sentence presents her love for Julien as true.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before it she writes, “comptez sur moi, vous me trouverez dévouée et ferme dans l'adversité.”\",\"supports\":\"Moderate performative support: the love avowal closes and ratifies an explicit assurance of devotion and steadfastness.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The letter urges Julien to come immediately and even to “sacrifiez tout, désertez s'il le faut.”\",\"supports\":\"Establishes crisis, urgency, and high emotional stakes, while supplying no independent evidence of reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"On Julien’s arrival, Mathilde “se précipita dans ses bras,” forgetting social propriety.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates her intense attachment and the seriousness of the written avowal, without independently changing its core force to E.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love statement in Chapter XXXV of Stendhal’s Le Rouge et le Noir.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its ordinary explicit love-statement form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal, and a moderate P score captures its commitment-inflected placement after a promise of steadfast devotion. Contextual urgency and passion can be described separately; no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
